    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d75">

                <pb facs="0188" n="82" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg82"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51W89 U.S.S.R./30: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1934-04-08T22:00:00+03:00">April 8, 1934—10
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 8—8:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>45. Supplementing my 43, April 8, 7 p.m., Umansky, chief of Foreign Office Press Department, called in
                    American correspondents this afternoon and gave lengthy prepared statement on
                    subject of Johnson bill and Soviet-American relations. It contained so many
                    phrases used to me by Litvinov that I
                    believed it was dictated by the latter. Umansky refused to allow quotation or attribution to any
                    official Soviet source. <hi rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Herald Tribune</hi> will carry story.</p>
                <p>If Department approves course suggested in my 43, April 8, 7 p.m., think it well
                    to refuse comment on this trial balloon.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
